  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The state of Georgia received $190M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date. The federal government made 3,297 awards in Georgia this fiscal year to date, and the average award size was $57.7K. Obligations fell 98.1% year over year. Historical context shows much larger annual totals in recent years: FY22 obligations were $8.9B, FY23 obligations were $11.5B, FY24 obligations were $10.7B, FY25 obligations were $12B, and FY26 obligations were $10.3B. The Department of Health and Human Services led buying in FY2027 to date with $68.9M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $66.6M and the Department of Homeland Security with $18.7M. FOUR SEASONS ENVIRONMENTAL INC was the largest vendor in FY2027 to date with $49.3M. Facilities Support Services was the largest industry by obligations in FY2027 to date with $51.8M. All figures are contract obligations, and current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What happened to federal contracting in Georgia this fiscal year?",
        "body": "The state of Georgia received $190M in obligations in FY2027 to date, down 98.1% year over year. The federal government issued 3,297 awards with an average award size of $57.7K. Recent fiscal years show much larger totals, including FY26 obligations of $10.3B and FY25 obligations of $12B. Current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who bought and who won the most federal dollars in Georgia?",
        "body": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $68.9M and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $66.6M in FY2027 to date, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $18.7M. FOUR SEASONS ENVIRONMENTAL INC received $49.3M and T47 INTERNATIONAL, INC. received $18.7M in FY2027 to date. Facilities Support Services led industries with $51.8M, and Janitorial Services totaled $18.7M."
      }
    ]
  },
